        "math_academy_id": "q-10563",
        "problem": "Calculate $\\int 100{tan}^{2}\u2061x\\,\\text{d}x.$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we select the identity that contains a ${tan}^{2}\u2061x$ term. So we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x={sec}^{2}\u2061x.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above identity gives\n\n$$\n{tan}^{2}\u2061x={sec}^{2}\u2061x-1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 100{tan}^{2}\u2061x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 100({sec}^{2}\u2061x-1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (100{sec}^{2}\u2061x-100)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =100\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x-100x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
